    text: "In many cities around the world, homes have become much more expensive over the past few decades, often rising far faster than wages. Economists point to several reasons. The first is land. People want to live where the jobs, schools and entertainment are, and the amount of land in the most popular areas is limited. As cities grow richer and more crowded, the value of that land rises. The second reason is building rules. In many places, planning laws restrict how many homes can be built, how tall buildings may be, or what kind of homes are allowed. These rules often have good reasons, such as protecting green spaces or historic streets, but they make it harder for supply to keep up with demand. Building also takes time, so even when prices rise, new homes arrive slowly. The third reason is interest rates. For much of the period from the 1980s to the early 2020s, interest rates fell, which made mortgages cheaper. When people can borrow more cheaply, they can afford to pay more for a home, and prices tend to rise. Other factors play a part too, such as population growth, smaller households, investors buying homes to rent out, and tax rules that favour home owners. High housing costs have serious effects. They can make it hard for young people to leave their parents' homes, push workers far from their jobs and widen the gap between those who own property and those who do not. Most economists agree that, in the long run, building more homes where people want to live is an important part of any solution."
